      title: "Two Societies at War",
      body: `## The balance of resources

On paper, the Union had overwhelming advantages. The twenty-three states that remained loyal, including the four slaveholding border states, held about 22 million people; the eleven Confederate states held about 9 million, of whom about 3.5 million were enslaved. The North had about 90 percent of the nation's manufacturing capacity, produced almost all of its firearms and iron, held most of its banking capital, and had more than twice as many miles of railroad. It controlled the navy and the merchant marine. And it had a functioning national government, with a treasury, a regular army, and diplomatic recognition.

The Confederacy had advantages too. It needed only to defend itself, not to conquer; to win, it had only to make the war too costly for the North to continue, as the American colonists had done to Britain. It could fight on interior lines, moving troops between threatened points faster than the Union could. It was vast—some 750,000 square miles—and an invading army would have to occupy it. Many of the most experienced officers of the old army, among them **Robert E. Lee**, had resigned to join it. Its white population was united, at least at first, in defense of its homes and its way of life. And its leaders believed that Britain and France, dependent on Southern cotton, would intervene on its behalf.

## Strategy

At the war's start, the Union's general-in-chief, the aging Winfield Scott, proposed the **Anaconda Plan**: a naval blockade of the Southern coast, combined with an advance down the Mississippi River to split the Confederacy, which would gradually squeeze the South into submission without a bloody invasion. Northern public opinion, impatient for a quick victory, mocked the plan; newspapers demanded an immediate march "On to Richmond!" In the end, the Union would follow much of Scott's strategy, but it would also require the invasion and conquest of the South.

The Confederacy's strategy was primarily defensive, but its president, **Jefferson Davis**, and its greatest general, Lee, believed in an "offensive-defensive": striking at invading armies, and occasionally carrying the war into the North, to win battles that would demoralize Northern voters.

## Mobilization

Both sides were unprepared. The regular U.S. Army numbered only about 16,000 men, most of them scattered in posts across the West. Both sides relied at first on volunteers, raised by states and local communities: companies of men from the same town, who elected their own officers and marched off to war amid parades and speeches. In the first months, enthusiasm was overwhelming. Both sides expected a short war.

## A people's contest

Lincoln explained the meaning of the war in his message to Congress on July 4, 1861. "This is essentially a People's contest," he said. "On the side of the Union, it is a struggle for maintaining in the world, that form, and substance of government, whose leading object is, to elevate the condition of men—to lift artificial weights from all shoulders—to clear the paths of laudable pursuit for all—to afford all, an unfettered start, and a fair chance, in the race of life." The war would test whether a democratic republic could survive the secession of a minority that lost an election. If it could not, he believed, government by the people would be discredited everywhere.

At the start, the Union's war aim was to restore the Union, not to abolish slavery. Lincoln needed the support of the slaveholding border states and of Northern Democrats, and Congress in July 1861 passed resolutions declaring that the war was not being waged to interfere with slavery. That would change, as you will see in Lesson III.

## Civil liberties

The war raised hard questions about civil liberties. In April 1861, with Washington surrounded by slave territory and pro-Confederate mobs in Baltimore attacking Union troops, Lincoln suspended the writ of **habeas corpus** along the railroad line between Philadelphia and Washington, allowing the military to arrest and hold suspected secessionists without trial. When a Maryland secessionist, John Merryman, was arrested, Chief Justice Taney, sitting as a circuit judge, ruled in *Ex parte Merryman* that only Congress could suspend the writ. Lincoln ignored him. "Are all the laws, but one, to go unexecuted, and the government itself go to pieces, lest that one be violated?" he asked Congress. Over the course of the war, Union authorities arrested perhaps 15,000 civilians, most of them in the border states and occupied South. The Confederacy also suspended habeas corpus and arrested dissenters. The tension between liberty and security in wartime would recur throughout American history.

## Diplomacy

The Confederacy's hopes for European intervention rested on "**King Cotton**." Southern leaders believed that Britain, whose textile mills depended on Southern cotton, would be forced to recognize the Confederacy and break the blockade. They even withheld cotton exports in 1861 to increase the pressure. The strategy failed. British mills had large stockpiles from the bumper crops of the late 1850s, and found new sources of cotton in Egypt and India. Britain also depended on Northern wheat. And British public opinion, especially among workers, was hostile to slavery.

The closest the war came to involving Britain was the **Trent affair** of November 1861, when a Union warship stopped a British mail steamer and seized two Confederate diplomats, James Mason and John Slidell. Britain demanded their release and sent troops to Canada. Lincoln, deciding on "one war at a time," released them. Britain and France recognized the Confederacy as a belligerent, but never as an independent nation. The skill of the American minister in London, **Charles Francis Adams**, the son of John Quincy Adams, helped keep it so.

## Two presidents

The war was also a contest of leadership. Davis was experienced, honest, and devoted to his cause, but he was rigid, quarrelsome, and in poor health, and he alienated many Confederate politicians and generals. Lincoln, with far less administrative experience, proved to be a master politician, able to hold together a fractious coalition of Republicans, War Democrats, and border-state Unionists, and to articulate the meaning of the war in words that still move readers. Historians have long argued that the difference in leadership was one reason for the outcome.

> **Pause and consider.** Lincoln called the war "a People's contest." What did he believe was at stake beyond the Union itself?`,
