      title: "The Home Fronts",
      body: `## The Union transformed

The war transformed the federal government. With Southern Democrats gone from Congress, the Republicans enacted much of the program they and the Whigs had long advocated. In 1862 alone, Congress passed:

- the **Homestead Act**, offering 160 acres of public land free to settlers who lived on and improved it for five years;
- the **Morrill Land-Grant College Act**, granting public lands to states to fund colleges teaching agriculture and the "mechanic arts," which created many of the great public universities;
- the **Pacific Railway Act**, which chartered companies to build a transcontinental railroad with generous land grants and loans;
- and higher protective tariffs.

To pay for the war, Congress enacted the first federal **income tax** and a host of excise taxes; sold bonds to ordinary citizens through the banker Jay Cooke; issued paper money, the "**greenbacks**," not backed by gold, under the Legal Tender Act of 1862; and created a system of nationally chartered banks in 1863–64. These measures created a national currency and greatly strengthened the federal government. By war's end, the federal government spent more each year than it had in its entire history before 1861.

## Northern politics

The war was not universally popular in the North. Democrats divided between "**War Democrats**," who supported the war to restore the Union, and "**Peace Democrats**," whom Republicans called "**Copperheads**," after the poisonous snake, who opposed emancipation, conscription, and the suppression of civil liberties, and demanded a negotiated peace. The most prominent Copperhead, the Ohio congressman Clement Vallandigham, was arrested by the military in 1863 for a speech denouncing the war, tried by a military commission, and banished to the Confederacy. The arrest caused an outcry, and Lincoln defended it in a famous letter: "Must I shoot a simple-minded soldier boy who deserts, while I must not touch a hair of a wiley agitator who induces him to desert?"

The Northern economy, after an initial slump, boomed. War contracts enriched manufacturers, and some made fortunes selling shoddy goods to the army. Inflation eroded workers' wages, provoking strikes. Farm production increased despite the loss of manpower, as women and children did more of the work and farmers bought mechanical reapers.

## Women and the war

Women on both sides took on new roles. They ran farms and businesses while men were away. They worked in arsenals and factories, sometimes dying in explosions. They organized soldiers' aid societies, sewed uniforms, rolled bandages, and raised money at great "Sanitary Fairs." Thousands served as nurses, overcoming the objections of army doctors who believed hospitals no place for women. The federal government hired women as clerks for the first time. Some women disguised themselves as men and enlisted; historians have identified several hundred. Others served as spies. The war expanded women's sense of their capacities, though it did not immediately expand their rights.

## The Confederacy's ordeal

The Confederacy faced far greater strains. Its leaders, champions of states' rights, found themselves building a centralized war state. In April 1862 the Confederacy passed the first conscription law in American history, a year before the Union. Its government seized food, horses, and enslaved laborers for the army through "impressment," and imposed a tax-in-kind, taking a tenth of farmers' produce. It built arsenals, factories, and mines, and regulated railroads and shipping. The historian Emory Thomas called this the "Confederate revolution": the South's war for states' rights produced a government more centralized than the one it had left.

Confederate finances collapsed. With little gold and few taxes, the government printed money, and inflation soared to some 9,000 percent by the war's end. The blockade, though porous, grew tighter year by year, and shortages of food, salt, cloth, and medicine spread. In April 1863 hundreds of women marched through **Richmond** demanding bread and broke into stores, until Davis himself climbed on a cart and threatened to have the militia fire on them.

## A rich man's war

Class resentment grew. The Confederate conscription law exempted one white man on every plantation with twenty or more enslaved people—the "**Twenty Negro Law**"—to supervise the enslaved and guard against revolt. To many non-slaveholding soldiers, it seemed proof that this was "a rich man's war and a poor man's fight." Desertion soared, especially in the later years of the war, as soldiers' families wrote of starvation at home. In the mountains of western North Carolina, eastern Tennessee, and northern Alabama, many white Southerners had always opposed secession; some fought for the Union, and Unionist and Confederate guerrillas waged a brutal war of their own. The historian Stephanie McCurry, in *Confederate Reckoning* (2010), showed how the Confederacy's attempt to build a nation for white male slaveholders was undermined by the very people it excluded: poor white women who demanded bread, and enslaved people who fled.

## The enslaved and the Confederacy

Slavery, the Confederacy's foundation, became one of its greatest weaknesses. The Confederacy depended on enslaved labor to grow food and build fortifications. But as Union armies approached, hundreds of thousands of enslaved people fled to Union lines, depriving the Confederacy of their labor and adding to the Union's strength. Those who remained worked less, defied overseers, and waited. Masters who fled before Union armies took the people they enslaved with them, "refugeeing" them to Texas and the interior. The institution the Confederacy had been founded to protect was disintegrating from within, as you will see in Lesson III.

> **Pause and consider.** Why did the Confederacy, founded on states' rights, become a more centralized state than the Union it had left?`,
